# Independent review: immutable recovery release storage receipt

Verdict: no blocking finding in the reviewed metadata/download-byte verification scope. All three final source snapshots match commit bfff8b3 byte-for-byte.

Independently checked the GitHub [release REST response](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/releases#get-a-release) and [asset metadata](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/assets#get-a-release-asset): the selected ID/URL/tag/draft/immutable/publication-date fields and asset name/ID/URL/state/size/digest fields match the documented API structure. The fixed three-asset profile is a NEPL3 contract rather than a general GitHub requirement. [Immutable release documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/concepts/supply-chain-security/immutable-releases) supports publishing a draft after all assets are attached; immutability does not prevent deletion of the whole release. The parser checks supplied metadata rather than performing that publication workflow.

Reviewed release.py, four tests and README against chapter 15's original-payload, immutable recovery storage and LKG separation. The verifier requires exact typed pins, one of each profile asset, bounded metadata/assets, strict boolean immutable/non-draft flags, release and asset endpoint/ID consistency, uploaded state, reported digest/size, and matching actual supplied bytes. Missing/duplicate assets or absent digests do not fall back to success. The resulting metadata digest binds the exact input response bytes.

Independent execution: four tests passed normally and with Python -O. A further 23-case probe checks every used required release/asset field missing, same-length corruption of each download, malformed typed asset pins, the 65,536/65,537-byte metadata boundary, and configured asset-limit exact/over cases. The asset boundary uses a reduced test limit instead of allocating 40 MiB; no full maximum-sized asset was exercised. commands.json, probe-results.json and raw outputs preserve execution.

The fixture intentionally contains a non-tar payload and an uninterpreted smoke JSON document. Their acceptance establishes storage-byte consistency only. The README accurately retains separate requirements for authenticated transport, original tar verification, identity/smoke semantics, tag-to-commit resolution, current publication/journal agreement and LKG promotion. No release download/publication, live GitHub authentication, real immutability enforcement or LKG acceptance was performed by this reviewer. No implementation edits were made.
